"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"][10]={"university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M10-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M10-L1 – Grid Structure and Equipment<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor: Electrical Power Networks<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Three-phase\nsystems<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Transformers<\/a>,\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Networks,\nlines and losses<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Convert a single-line diagram\ninto a computational node-branch model and distinguish the functions of\nthe equipment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From the interconnected grid to the single-line diagram<\/h2>\n<p>An electricity grid connects generators, storage systems and\nconsumers via lines and transformers. Transmission networks carry power\nover longer distances; distribution networks provide connections to\nregional and local consumers. Distribution networks also contain\ngenerators. The classification therefore does not necessarily describe\nthe direction of power flow.<\/p>\n<p>A single-line diagram represents the three conductors of a\nthree-phase system as one connection. For balanced sinusoidal operation,\na per-phase model using complex RMS values is sufficient; total power,\nby contrast, refers to all three phases. We use the line-to-line voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{LL}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the line current\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor balanced loads,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>I<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|S|=\\sqrt{3}U_{LL}I,\\qquad P=|S|\\cos\\varphi,\\qquad Q=|S|\\sin\\varphi.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A node groups ideal electrical connection points. A branch connects\ntwo nodes via an item of equipment. Switching states determine which\nbranches actually conduct. This distinction makes it possible to derive\nseveral operating states from the same network diagram.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Equipment and equivalent models<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Equipment<\/th>\n<th>Function<\/th>\n<th>Model for steady-state operation<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Infeed / generator<\/td>\n<td>Supply power and voltage<\/td>\n<td>Reference, PV or PQ node<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overhead line or cable<\/td>\n<td>Transfer power<\/td>\n<td>Series impedance and, if needed, shunt admittances<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transformer<\/td>\n<td>Connect voltage levels<\/td>\n<td>Turns ratio, leakage impedance, magnetisation if necessary<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Busbar<\/td>\n<td>Connect terminals<\/td>\n<td>Common node<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Circuit breaker<\/td>\n<td>Switch operating and fault currents<\/td>\n<td>Open or closed connection<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Load<\/td>\n<td>Draw active and, if applicable, reactive power<\/td>\n<td>Constant power, current or impedance<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>For a first approximation, shunt admittances may be neglected for\nshort lines. This is not generally permissible for long lines and cables\nwith substantial capacitance. In the later model, we use only series\nimpedances\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=R+jX<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe resistance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncauses active power losses, while the reactance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nparticularly affects reactive power exchange and voltage variation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_027_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A three-node grid with an infeed at node 1, two load nodes and three lines.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: An infeed at node 1 supplies two load nodes through\nthree branches in a three-node grid; the closed ring provides\nalternative paths.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The model shown reduces an upstream supply to node 1 and two load\nareas to nodes 2 and 3. The three lines form a closed ring. Power flow\ncan be distributed along several paths. Line 2–3 may also carry almost\nno load even though it is relevant for an alternative supply.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Voltage level and current demand<\/h2>\n<p>At 10 MW of active power and a lagging power factor of 0.8, the\napparent power is 12.5 MVA. At 20 kV,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>360.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=360.8\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the same power is ideally transmitted at 110 kV, it is only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>65.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">65.6\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor hypothetical equal conductor resistances of 1 Ω per phase,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>R<\/mi><msup><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_V=3RI^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\napproximately 390.6 kW versus 12.9 kW. The subscript V denotes losses.\nThis is a comparison at equal resistances, not a design for real lines.\nInsulation, cross-section, construction, transformer losses and reactive\npower alter the overall balance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Topology and availability<\/h2>\n<p>In a radial network there is exactly one path between the infeed and\neach load. An interrupted line may disconnect a downstream part of the\nnetwork. A ring may provide additional paths. Initially, this redundancy\nsays only something about connectivity. It does not guarantee that the\nremaining branches can carry the current or maintain voltages within the\nintended range.<\/p>\n<p>An operating calculation therefore requires not only the connections\nbut also the impedances, power data, reference voltage and limits. A\nsingle-line diagram alone determines neither power flow nor protection\nsettings. Switching operations and work on real power networks are\noutside the scope of this learning model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A balanced load draws 6 MW at 10 kV and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos\\varphi=0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDetermine the line current.<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>384.9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=6\\cdot10^6/(\\sqrt3\\cdot10^4\\cdot0.9)=384.9\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which loads remain connected to node 1 after interruption of line\n1–3 in the triangle shown?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> Both, via 1–2 and 1–2–3 respectively.\nPermissible currents and voltages must be checked separately.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can a short line branch be represented as a series impedance in\na power-flow model, whereas a long cable often cannot?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> The capacitive charging current of a long line\nmay be significant; the purely series model omits it completely.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further study:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Equivalent\nparameters, base quantities and the nodal matrix<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.ieh.kit.edu/2448_bee.php\">KIT – Electrical\nPower Networks, lecture contents<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · EEN syllabus, item 1: power\nsupply structure; curriculum comparison. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://onlinecourses-archive.nptel.ac.in/noc18_ee16/preview\">IIT\nKharagpur / NPTEL – Power System Analysis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Course Layout, weeks\n2–4: line parameters and per-unit system; lecture contents not reviewed.\n· Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M10-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M10-L2 – Equivalent Parameters, Base Quantities and the Nodal Matrix<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor: Electrical Power Networks<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Grid\nstructure<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Complex\nimpedance<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Systems of\nlinear equations<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Define three-phase base\nquantities consistently and construct a complex nodal admittance matrix\nfrom line data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Per-unit as a dimensionless reference system<\/h2>\n<p>Networks with multiple voltage levels can be simplified by using\nnormalised quantities. We choose a common three-phase base power\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a line-to-line base voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor each voltage level. This gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>U<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_B=\\frac{S_B}{\\sqrt3 U_B},\\qquad Z_B=\\frac{U_B^2}{S_B},\\qquad Y_B=\\frac1{Z_B}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The voltage of the per-phase model is normalised by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_B/\\sqrt3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the current by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe complex total power is then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><msup><mi>i<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=v i^*<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe factor of 3 is already accounted for by the choice of bases.\nLower-case letters here denote dimensionless per-unit quantities (pu),\nand the asterisk denotes complex conjugation. Physical quantities are\nrecovered by multiplying by their respective bases.<\/p>\n<p>Example:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_B=10\\,\\mathrm{MVA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_B=20\\,\\mathrm{kV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>288.675<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_B=288.675\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_B=40\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA line with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=0.8+j2\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntherefore has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=0.02+j0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA voltage of 19.2 kV corresponds to a magnitude of 0.96 pu.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transformers and change of base<\/h2>\n<p>If the voltage bases on both sides are chosen in the ratio of the\nideal rated transformation, the ideal transformer has a transformation\nratio of unity in the per-unit model (without phase shift). Its leakage\nimpedance remains. Off-nominal tap positions or phase shifts must be\nmodelled separately.<\/p>\n<p>An impedance already expressed in per-unit is converted to a\ndifferent base using<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mfrac><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_\\mathrm{neu}=z_\\mathrm{alt}\\frac{S_{B,\\mathrm{neu}}}{S_{B,\\mathrm{alt}}}\\left(\\frac{U_{B,\\mathrm{alt}}}{U_{B,\\mathrm{neu}}}\\right)^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The subscripts neu and alt denote the new and old bases respectively.\nExample: a transformer rated at 5 MVA has a leakage reactance of 0.06 pu\non its own base. With the same voltage base and a 10 MVA system base, it\nbecomes 0.12 pu. The physical impedance does not change.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Constructing the nodal admittance matrix<\/h2>\n<p>We use the three-node network from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L1<\/a>,\nwith the following per-unit series impedances:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Line<\/th>\n<th><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(pu)<\/th>\n<th><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=1/z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(pu)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1–2<\/td>\n<td>0.02 + j0.05<\/td>\n<td>6.8966 − j17.2414<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2–3<\/td>\n<td>0.03 + j0.06<\/td>\n<td>6.6667 − j13.3333<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1–3<\/td>\n<td>0.04 + j0.08<\/td>\n<td>5 − j10<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The current flowing from node\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninto a line branch is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_{km}=y_{km}(v_k-v_m)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe count the resulting current injection\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas positive into the line network. Kirchhoff’s current law gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><munder><munder><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>13<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>13<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>23<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>23<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>13<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>23<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>13<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>23<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟<\/mo><\/munder><mi>Y<\/mi><\/munder><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{pmatrix}i_1\\\\i_2\\\\i_3\\end{pmatrix}=\n\\underbrace{\\begin{pmatrix}\ny_{12}+y_{13}&amp;-y_{12}&amp;-y_{13}\\\\\n-y_{12}&amp;y_{12}+y_{23}&amp;-y_{23}\\\\\n-y_{13}&amp;-y_{23}&amp;y_{13}+y_{23}\n\\end{pmatrix}}_{Y}\n\\begin{pmatrix}v_1\\\\v_2\\\\v_3\\end{pmatrix}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_028_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Line 2–3 contributes +y23 to diagonal entries 22 and 33 and −y23 to off-diagonal entries 23 and 32 of the matrix.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The branch between nodes 2 and 3 adds its admittance\npositively to both diagonal entries and negatively to the two symmetric\noff-diagonal entries.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Each line adds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto two diagonal entries and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto two off-diagonal entries. Without shunt admittances, the rows sum to\nzero. It follows that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y\\mathbf 1=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nthe common additive offset of all complex node potentials is not\ndetermined by the specified branch currents alone. In this model we\ntherefore fix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_1=1+j0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand, for known currents, solve only for the remaining nodes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From specified currents to specified powers<\/h2>\n<p>The nodal matrix describes the linear line network. Loads with\nconstant complex power, however, are nonlinear:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_k=(s_k/v_k)^*<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA negative power value denotes net consumption here. As the voltage\nmagnitude falls, the current magnitude rises for the same apparent\npower. An iterative power-flow calculation is therefore required. The\nnodal matrix remains constant as long as the topology is unchanged; the\ncurrent injections are updated.<\/p>\n<p>When a line is disconnected, its entire contribution must be removed\nfrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf this creates an island without a reference node, the reduced equation\nof this model can no longer be solved using an invertible matrix. Such\nan island requires its own supply model; it is not represented by\nassigning it a numerical zero voltage.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the base impedance at 100 MVA and 110 kV?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>110<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>121<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_B=(110\\,\\mathrm{kV})^2/(100\\,\\mathrm{MVA})=121\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Determine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mn>23<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_{23}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof the three-node network.<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>23<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6.6667<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>13.3333<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-y_{23}=-6.6667+j13.3333<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npu.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which matrix entry changes if an additional shunt admittance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mn>0.02<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j0.02<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis connected at node 3?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> Only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mn>33<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_{33}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincreases by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mn>0.02<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j0.02<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npu; the sum of row 3 is then no longer zero.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further study:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Steady-state\npower flow<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://onlinecourses-archive.nptel.ac.in/noc18_ee16/preview\">IIT\nKharagpur / NPTEL – Power System Analysis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Course Layout, weeks\n5–6: load flow analysis; lecture contents not reviewed. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://matpower.app/manual/matpower/NetworkEquations.html\">MATPOWER\n– Network Equations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · MATPOWER manual §3.6, eqs. (3.8), (3.17):\nnodal admittance and AC power balance. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M10-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M10-L3 – Steady-State Power Flow<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor: Electrical Power Networks<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Nodal\nmatrix<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">AC\npower<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish the node types of an\nAC power flow, formulate the power balance and solve a small power-flow\nproblem iteratively.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Node types and sign conventions<\/h2>\n<p>In the balanced, steady-state AC model, the complex power injection\nat each node is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_k=p_k+jq_k=v_k\\left(\\sum_mY_{km}v_m\\right)^*.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenote generation minus consumption. Generally, two of the four\nquantities\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p,q,|v|,\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare specified; the remaining two follow from the network. The angle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis relative to a common reference angle.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type<\/th>\n<th>Specified<\/th>\n<th>Calculated<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Reference node (slack)<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert v\\rvert,\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p,q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PV node (voltage-controlled injection)<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p,\\lvert v\\rvert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q,\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PQ node<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p,q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert v\\rvert,\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>PV here denotes the node type, not photovoltaics. When a reactive\npower limit is reached, a PV node can be switched to fixed reactive\npower in the model. The voltage then becomes a calculated quantity. The\nslack node accounts mathematically for the remaining balance, including\nlosses; this does not imply unlimited real generation capacity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Voltage drop and power flow<\/h2>\n<p>For a line without shunt admittance,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_{km}=(v_k-v_m)/z_{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe powers at both ends are counted positive into the branch:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><msubsup><mi>i<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>*<\/mo><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{km}=v_k i_{km}^*<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{mk}=v_m(-i_{km})^*<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTheir sum is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{km}+s_{mk}=z_{km}|i_{km}|^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The real part is the active power loss. For small voltage changes and\na receiving-end demand of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_L+jq_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe difference in voltage magnitudes can be approximated as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(rp_L+xq_L)/|v_m|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis follows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_k=v_m+zi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the projection onto the receiving-end voltage phasor. An inductive\nload can therefore increase the voltage drop even with unchanged active\npower.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example with two PQ nodes<\/h2>\n<p>Use the lines from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L2<\/a>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_1=1+j0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.6<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_2=-0.6-j0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>0.15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_3=-0.4-j0.15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince nodes 2 and 3 are PQ nodes, we can use the reduced matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_{LL}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nformed by rows and columns 2 and 3. The column vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_{L1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncouples in the reference. From\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_{LL}v_L+Y_{L1}v_1=i_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe obtain the fixed-point iteration<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>Y<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msubsup><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_L^{(n+1)}=Y_{LL}^{-1}\\left[\\left(\\frac{s_L}{v_L^{(n)}}\\right)^*-Y_{L1}v_1\\right].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The element-wise division represents the two separate PQ nodes. Start\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_2=v_3=1+j0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt each iteration, solve a system of linear equations and then check the\ncomplex power mismatch\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\max_{k=2,3}|v_k(Yv)_k^*-s_k|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe voltage change alone does not determine when to stop.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Node<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Magnitude (pu)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Angle<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(pu)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(pu)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.00000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0°<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.01654<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.43775<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.97358<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">−1.490°<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">−0.60000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">−0.25000<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.97242<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">−1.508°<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">−0.40000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">−0.15000<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_029_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Calculated voltage magnitudes of 1.00000, 0.97358 and 0.97242 pu and the magnitudes of the three branch currents in the base case.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: In the calculated base-case power flow, voltage\nmagnitudes fall from 1.00000 pu at the infeed to 0.97358 and 0.97242 pu\nat the load nodes.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>With a base power of 10 MVA, 10.1654 MW of injection supplies 10 MW\nof load. The loss of approximately 165.4 kW is independently checked\nusing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum r|i|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe reactive power requirement of the lines is around 0.3775 Mvar. The\nline currents in branches 1–2, 2–3 and 1–3 are 0.68388, 0.01801 and\n0.42350 pu. With unrounded data, the solution reaches a power mismatch\nbelow\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{-10}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npu after seven iterations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limitations of power-flow calculations<\/h2>\n<p>A converged power flow is a mathematical solution of the steady-state\nequations. It does not automatically satisfy current, voltage or\ngeneration limits. Depending on the network and loading, the simple\nfixed-point iteration may converge slowly or fail. This does not prove\nthat no solution exists. Newton–Raphson methods instead use a Jacobian\nmatrix of the power mismatches and solve linearised correction\nequations. They too require sensible initial values and a model\ncheck.<\/p>\n<p>The voltage angles of the example network are small. A DC power flow\ncan provide a simplified active power flow under additional assumptions\n(small angles, nearly constant voltage magnitudes and negligible\nresistances). Here, however, it does not replace the AC calculation,\nbecause reactive power, voltage drop and losses are to be\ninvestigated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A load of 4 MW and a local generator of 3 MW are located at the same\nnode. The load requires 1 Mvar, and the generator supplies no reactive\npower. What are the node data on a 10 MVA base?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=-0.1-j0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npu.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=0.02+j0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npu and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|i|=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npu, determine the active power losses on a 10 MVA base.<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.02<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>0.8<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>128<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.02\\cdot0.8^2\\cdot10\\,\\mathrm{MW}=128\\,\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can node 1 not be modelled simultaneously with constant power\ninjection and a specified complex voltage?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> Its power output must make up the difference\nbetween load, the other injections and losses; an additional fixed power\nwould generally overdetermine the system of equations.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further study:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Network\nfaults and protection principles<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Reproducible\npower-flow calculation<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://matpower.app/manual/matpower/ACPowerFlow.html\">MATPOWER –\nAC Power Flow<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 4.1: reference, PV and PQ buses in AC power flow.\n· Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://matpower.app/manual/matpower/NetworkEquations.html\">MATPOWER\n– Network Equations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · MATPOWER manual §3.6, eqs. (3.8), (3.17):\nnodal admittance and AC power balance. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://onlinecourses-archive.nptel.ac.in/noc18_ee16/preview\">IIT\nKharagpur / NPTEL – Power System Analysis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Course Layout, weeks\n5–8: load flow and optimal operation; lecture contents not reviewed. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M10-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M10-L4 – Grid Faults and Protection Principles<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor: Electrical Power Networks<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Equivalent\ncircuits<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Three-phase\nsystems<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Power\nflow<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate a symmetrical\nthree-phase short circuit using an equivalent model and distinguish the\nroles of fault detection, disconnection and selectivity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Normal and fault operation<\/h2>\n<p>A short circuit arises from an unintended low-impedance connection.\nCompared with normal power flow, the network conditions change\nsubstantially. A symmetrical three-phase fault can be investigated using\na single-phase equivalent circuit. Unbalanced faults (such as\nsingle-phase earth faults) require further models, for example\npositive-, negative- and zero-sequence networks with the appropriate\nneutral earthing arrangements. These are not calculated here.<\/p>\n<p>In the simple fault-current model, the supplying network as seen from\nthe fault location is represented by a Thévenin voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{\\mathrm{Th}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand an internal impedance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_{\\mathrm{Th}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe voltage is a per-phase RMS phasor; the fault resistance or fault\nimpedance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis specified per phase. Thus<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline I_f=\\frac{\\underline U_{\\mathrm{Th}}}{Z_{\\mathrm{Th}}+Z_f}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Realistic initial short-circuit currents require appropriate source\nand machine models, the pre-fault operating state and assumptions\nconsistent with relevant standards. The following numerical experiment\nassumes a prescribed ideal source behind a constant impedance. Neither\nthe peak short-circuit current nor a time-dependent decay is\ncalculated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Numerical example for a 20 kV network<\/h2>\n<p>Choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_B=10\\,\\mathrm{MVA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_B=20\\,\\mathrm{kV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_\\mathrm{Th}=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npu and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>0.10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_\\mathrm{Th}=0.02+j0.10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npu. This is an independent fault example, not a short-circuit\ncalculation for the ideal slack model from M10-L3. For a bolted fault\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_f=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>),<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>0.02<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>0.10<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.9231<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>9.6154<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.8058<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_f=\\frac1{0.02+j0.10}=1.9231-j9.6154,\\qquad |i_f|=9.8058.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>288.675<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_B=288.675\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.831<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|I_f|\\approx2.831\\,\\mathrm{kA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe conventional apparent short-circuit power referred to the nominal\nvoltage is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>98.06<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt3 U_B|I_f|\\approx98.06\\,\\mathrm{MVA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is not the power dissipated in the ideal fault resistance: for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_f=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe fault voltage is zero. If a real fault resistance of 0.10 pu is\nassumed,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|i_f|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfalls to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>0.12<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>0.10<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.4018<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt{0.12^2+0.10^2}=6.4018<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npu (1.848 kA).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Fault detection and selective disconnection<\/h2>\n<p>A protection system combines measured quantities, decision logic and\na switching device. Overcurrent protection responds to elevated\ncurrents. Differential protection compares the currents entering and\nleaving a defined zone, taking measurement and transformation ratios\ninto account. Distance protection uses the apparent impedance derived\nfrom voltage and current measurements as an indication of the fault\nlocation. The function depends on network type, source configuration and\nmeasurement conditions.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_030_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Radial supply with an upstream backup circuit breaker and two outgoing feeders, of which only the faulty one is disconnected by its local breaker.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Local protection isolates only the faulty feeder in\nthis simplified radial grid, while the healthy feeder remains\nconnected.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Selectivity means isolating the affected area as precisely as\npossible. In a radial arrangement, downstream protection can operate\nfirst; upstream protection serves as backup. As a purely conceptual\nexample, assume a clearing time of 0.15 s for the downstream device and\n0.45 s for the upstream device. The time-grading interval is 0.30 s.\nThese illustrative figures are not usable protection settings: actual\nprotection characteristics, tolerances, breaker operating times and\nminimum fault currents must be assessed together. In a ring with supply\nfrom both sides, simple time grading is often insufficient.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limitations and safety function<\/h2>\n<p>The maximum fault current is important for the stresses imposed on\nequipment. The minimum fault current, by contrast, is relevant to\nwhether protection detects a fault at all. Current-limited power\nelectronic converters can supply far less fault current than\nconventional synchronous machines; their fault response then depends on\nthe control and protection design. A power-flow calculation with an\nideal slack bus does not represent this behaviour.<\/p>\n<p>The loss of a line branch after disconnection constitutes a new\nsteady-state network case. The fault itself and the transients during\ndisconnection are different modelling tasks. A simple short-circuit\ncurrent calculation cannot establish authorisation to carry out work,\nselect a real protection device or verify an installation against\nstandards.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Halve the entire Thévenin impedance in the bolted-fault example.\nWhat happens to the current?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> Its magnitude doubles to approximately 5.66 kA\nwith the source voltage unchanged.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a smaller fault current not advantageous in every\nrespect?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> It reduces equipment stress but can fall below\nthe pickup threshold of overcurrent protection.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which part of the network does the downstream breaker disconnect in\nthe radial system shown?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> Only the faulty feeder; the other feeder\nremains connected if selective disconnection succeeds. Backup\ndisconnection would disconnect more loads.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further study:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Network\nplanning using a model example<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.ieh.kit.edu/2448_bee.php\">KIT – Electrical\nPower Networks, lecture contents<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · EEN syllabus, item 5: network\nfaults; curriculum comparison. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://onlinecourses-archive.nptel.ac.in/noc18_ee16/preview\">IIT\nKharagpur / NPTEL – Power System Analysis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Course Layout, weeks\n9–11: short circuits and unbalanced faults; lecture contents not\nreviewed. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://nptel.ac.in/courses/108101039\">IIT Bombay / NPTEL –\nPower System Protection, modules 1 and 4–10<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Power System\nProtection course overview, IIT Bombay: topic classification; teaching\nmaterials and syllabus not reviewed. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M10-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M10-L5 – Network Planning Using a Model Example<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor: Electrical Power Networks<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Nodal\nmatrix<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Power\nflow<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Grid\nfaults<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Computational\nexperiments<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate the behaviour of a\nmodel network under changing load, local generation and individual line\noutages, and evaluate it against explicit operating limits.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Planning question and model assumptions<\/h2>\n<p>The three-node grid from the previous lessons is intended to supply\nboth load areas even when demand changes. We distinguish power demand\nfrom available network capacity. A higher load current can exceed\nthermal limits, while unfavourable impedance can cause the voltage to\nbecome too low before that happens.<\/p>\n<p>All quantities are referred to 10 MVA and 20 kV. The illustrative\nrequirements chosen for this example are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.95<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.95\\le |v_k|\\le1.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npu and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|i_{km}|\\le1.0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npu (equivalent to 288.7 A). These are neither a voltage band prescribed\nby a standard nor an actual line rating. Node 1 remains ideally fixed at\n1 pu. All lines have only series impedances, and the loads are modelled\nas constant PQ nodes. There are no tap changers, source limits or\ndynamic processes.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Branch<\/th>\n<th>Impedance (pu)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1–2<\/td>\n<td>0.02 + j0.05<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2–3<\/td>\n<td>0.03 + j0.06<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1–3<\/td>\n<td>0.04 + j0.08<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The initial loads are 6 MW and 2.5 Mvar at node 2 and 4 MW and 1.5\nMvar at node 3. The increased-load case raises both loads by 50%. An\nadditional generator at node 3 injects 6 MW and 0 Mvar in the\nincreased-load case. In the model it is a fixed PQ injection;\nconsumption at the same node remains unchanged. The three outage\nscenarios each remove exactly one line from the original base operating\ncase.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reproducible calculation<\/h2>\n<p>The fixed-point iteration from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L3<\/a>\nis implemented with Python and NumPy. Node numbers in the code start at\nzero. The power balance and the branch currents are calculated for each\nvariant from the resulting network voltage; source power is not\nprescribed. The original German output labels are retained in the code:\n<em>Basis<\/em> denotes the base case, <em>Mehrlast<\/em> increased load,\n<em>Einspeisung<\/em> generation injection and <em>Ausfall<\/em> outage.\nThe error messages indicate voltage near zero and failure to\nconverge.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>lines <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.02<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"ot\">.05j<\/span>), (<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.03<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"ot\">.06j<\/span>),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>         (<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.04<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"ot\">.08j<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> solve(branches, s, tol<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-10<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    Y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.zeros((<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>), dtype<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">complex<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> a, b, z <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> branches:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>z<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        Y[a, a] <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> y<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        Y[b, b] <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> y<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        Y[a, b] <span class=\"op\">-=<\/span> y<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        Y[b, a] <span class=\"op\">-=<\/span> y<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    s <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array(s, dtype<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">complex<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    v <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.ones(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, dtype<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">complex<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> n <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">501<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> np.<span class=\"bu\">any<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(v) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1e-12<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;Spannung nahe null&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        v <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.linalg.solve(Y[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>:, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>:],<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                            np.conj(s<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>v)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>Y[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>:, <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        V <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.r_[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"ot\">0j<\/span>, v]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        S <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> V<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.conj(Y<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>V)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        err <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(S[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>:]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>s))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> err <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> tol:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">break<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;Keine Konvergenz&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-28\"><a href=\"#cb1-28\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    I <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>((V[a]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>V[b])<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>z) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> a, b, z <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> branches]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-29\"><a href=\"#cb1-29\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    loss <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(z.real<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>i<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>i <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> (_, _, z), i <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">zip<\/span>(branches, I))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-30\"><a href=\"#cb1-30\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(S.real.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>()<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>loss) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1e-9<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-31\"><a href=\"#cb1-31\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> V, S, I, loss, n, err<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-32\"><a href=\"#cb1-32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-33\"><a href=\"#cb1-33\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>base <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"ot\">.25j<\/span>, <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"ot\">.15j<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-34\"><a href=\"#cb1-34\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>heavy <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.9<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"ot\">.375j<\/span>, <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"ot\">.225j<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-35\"><a href=\"#cb1-35\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>cases <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [(<span class=\"st\">&quot;Basis&quot;<\/span>, lines, base),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-36\"><a href=\"#cb1-36\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>         (<span class=\"st\">&quot;Mehrlast&quot;<\/span>, lines, heavy),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-37\"><a href=\"#cb1-37\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>         (<span class=\"st\">&quot;Einspeisung&quot;<\/span>, lines, [heavy[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>], <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"ot\">.225j<\/span>])]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-38\"><a href=\"#cb1-38\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> k, name <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">enumerate<\/span>([<span class=\"st\">&quot;1-2&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;2-3&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;1-3&quot;<\/span>]):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-39\"><a href=\"#cb1-39\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    cases.append((<span class=\"st\">&quot;Ausfall &quot;<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> name,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-40\"><a href=\"#cb1-40\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                  [z <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> i, z <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">enumerate<\/span>(lines) <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> i <span class=\"op\">!=<\/span> k], base))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-41\"><a href=\"#cb1-41\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> name, branches, s <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> cases:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-42\"><a href=\"#cb1-42\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    V, S, I, loss, n, err <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> solve(branches, s)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-43\"><a href=\"#cb1-43\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    ok <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">min<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(V)) <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.95<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(V)) <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1.05<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(I) <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-44\"><a href=\"#cb1-44\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(name, <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(V), <span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(I), loss<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10000<\/span>, n, err, ok)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>A singular matrix, for example if an isolated island has no reference\nnode, raises an exception. A larger planning tool would need to check\nthe connected components beforehand. The example covers connected\nnetworks only. The internal power residual bounds the numerical\ndeviation, not the physical modelling error.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evaluation of the six operating cases<\/h2>\n<p>With unrounded inputs, the calculation produces the following values.\nLosses are converted to kW. All solutions achieve a complex power\nresidual smaller than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{-10}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npu.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-15\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-15\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-15\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-15\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-15\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Operating case<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert v_2\\rvert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(pu)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert v_3\\rvert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(pu)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\max \\lvert i\\rvert<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(pu)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Loss (kW)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Iter.<\/th>\n<th>Limits<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Base<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.97358<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.97242<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.68388<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">165.38<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7<\/td>\n<td>satisfied<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Increased load<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.95952<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.95773<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.04105<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">383.30<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9<\/td>\n<td>current<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Increased load + injection<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.96581<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.97244<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.79600<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">188.40<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8<\/td>\n<td>satisfied<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outage 1–2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.87709<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.91529<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.20710<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">747.60<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">13<\/td>\n<td>voltage, current<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outage 2–3<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.97453<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.97080<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.66699<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">166.43<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7<\/td>\n<td>satisfied<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outage 1–3<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.95642<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.93370<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.13712<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">321.41<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9<\/td>\n<td>voltage, current<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_031_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Minimum voltage and maximum line current for six load and outage scenarios compared with the freely chosen limits.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Six load and outage scenarios are compared with\nmodel limits of 0.95 pu minimum voltage and 1.0 pu maximum current; red\npoints indicate violations.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The increased-load case stays within the chosen voltage band but\nexceeds the current limit. Local generation improves both quantities\neven though it supplies no reactive power. This is due to the changed\nbranch currents, not a fixed regulated node voltage. The slack bus still\nsupplies about 9.1884 MW in this case; the net active power demand is 9\nMW. With even greater generation injection, power-flow directions can\nreverse and voltages can become too high.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Redundancy and qualitative robustness<\/h2>\n<p>All three individual outages leave both load nodes connected to the\nreference. Nevertheless, two outage cases fail to satisfy the prescribed\nlimits. The ring is therefore topologically redundant but is not\nadequately sized at this operating point to pass this simplified N-1\ncheck. Here N-1 means the loss of an individual line branch with\nunchanged loads and no remedial actions; this is not a complete\noperational security analysis.<\/p>\n<p>Possible planning variants include lower line impedances, higher\npermissible current through appropriate equipment, local reactive power\nprovision or an alternative supply. Each variant changes the model in a\ndifferent way and should first be tested individually, then in\ncombination. Raising the current limit alone does not eliminate a\nviolation of the voltage band. A new source also requires consideration\nof availability, protection functions and other operating cases. A\ntime-dependent generation profile is covered in the planned module\nM11.<\/p>\n<p>The investigation quantifies steady-state operating limits, not\ntransient stability, protection coordination or actual outage\nprobabilities. A real decision requires measurement data, equipment\ndata, additional load and generation scenarios and applicable technical\nrequirements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What minimum voltages occur in outage cases 1–2 and 1–3? Which\nbranches carry the maximum current?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> 0.87709 pu at node 2, maximum current on 1–3;\n0.93370 pu at node 3, maximum current on 1–2.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The increased-load case has a maximum current of 1.04105 pu.\nApproximately how many amperes is this?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.04105<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>288.675<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.04105\\cdot288.675\\,\\mathrm A=300.5\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor about 11.9 A above the model limit.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which conclusion changes if only the voltage band, but not the\ncurrent limit, is checked?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> The increased-load case would incorrectly be\nrated acceptable. Even a steady-state mathematical solution with no\nresidual error does not guarantee compliance with further operating\nrequirements.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Run the base case with a stricter tolerance of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{-12}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand compare the voltages and losses.<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> The rounded values reported remain unchanged.\nThe residual check becomes stricter and the number of iterations\nincreases. Such a repetition investigates only the numerical error, not\nthe quality of the input data.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further study:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;page=study-path&amp;section=S3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Study\npathway and further elective modules<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.ieh.kit.edu/2448_bee.php\">KIT – Electrical\nPower Networks, lecture contents<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · EEN syllabus, items 3 and 4:\nnetwork calculation and optimal power flow; curriculum comparison. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://matpower.app/manual/matpower/ACPowerFlow.html\">MATPOWER –\nAC Power Flow<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Manual §4.1: AC power balance and bus types;\nlimitations of ordinary power flow, methodological aspect. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://matpower.app/manual/matpower/StandardACOPF.html\">MATPOWER\n– Standard AC OPF<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 6.1: optimization variables, cost function and\npower balance; further reading. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
